      "instructions": "1-6: Complete the inspection booking notes. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer.\n7-10: Choose the correct letter, A, B or C.",
      "transcript": "Jasper Tait: Good morning, Otago Hearth Inspections. Jasper speaking. How can I help today?\nMireille Corbin: Hello. I would like to book the annual inspection for the heater in my house well before next winter, when we will use it regularly.\nJasper Tait: Certainly. Is the appliance an open fireplace, an enclosed insert, or a freestanding wood burner?\nMireille Corbin: It is a freestanding wood burner. The previous owner called it a stove, but it stands clear of the brick wall rather than sitting inside a fireplace.\nJasper Tait: Thanks. What suburb should I put on the booking? We group appointments by area so the inspector is not crossing the city repeatedly.\nMireille Corbin: Mornington. The street address is twelve Lorne Street, but Mornington is the suburb you need for the route.\nJasper Tait: And what fuel do you normally burn? Some households mix manufactured logs with ordinary firewood.\nMireille Corbin: We tried manufactured logs once, then stopped. We now use dry hardwood, stored under cover, so please record hardwood as the main fuel.\nJasper Tait: The inspector may need to examine the flue from inside the roof space. Where is the access hatch?\nMireille Corbin: It is inside the hall cupboard. Someone suggested moving it to the laundry, but that work never happened, so the hall cupboard is still correct.\nJasper Tait: I can offer Monday the seventeenth of November at nine thirty, or Wednesday the nineteenth after lunch.\nMireille Corbin: Wednesday clashes with work. Let us take 17 November at nine thirty, please.\nJasper Tait: Afterwards we can post a printed certificate, send an ordinary email attachment, or place the signed report behind a secure link.\nMireille Corbin: A secure link would be best. Our insurer accepts the signed digital version, and I would rather not have the report sitting in an unprotected email.\nJasper Tait: That completes the booking details. I will now explain what the standard visit covers and what you should do before we arrive.\nJasper Tait: We begin with a visual check of the appliance, hearth, visible joints and accessible flue sections. An inspection camera is not used automatically.\nMireille Corbin: I wondered whether I should pay for the camera now because a bird once nested near the chimney.\nJasper Tait: Not yet. If the visual check suggests a blockage or hidden damage, the inspector will discuss the camera with you before adding it. That avoids an unnecessary charge.\nMireille Corbin: Why is the morning appointment longer than the afternoon slot I saw online?\nJasper Tait: It is not longer. We chose morning because the burner must be completely cold, and an early visit makes that easier after an overnight shutdown.\nMireille Corbin: I saw both a brief condition note and a detailed compliance certificate on the website. Which one have I actually booked?\nJasper Tait: The detailed certificate. The brief note is for landlords who only want a visible-condition record; your insurer asked for measurements and photographs as well.\nMireille Corbin: Is there anything I should move before the inspector arrives?\nJasper Tait: Please clear the hall cupboard so the roof hatch opens fully. Leave the heavy stove in place, remove loose ash only when cold, and keep pets away from the work area.\nMireille Corbin: Fine. The burner will be cold, the cupboard will be clear, and I will use the secure link when the detailed certificate is ready.\nJasper Tait: Perfect. You will receive a reminder the day before. If the weather prevents a safe roof check, we will contact you rather than leave an incomplete certificate.",
