      "transcript": "Bronte Hales: Welcome to Harbour Clay Studio. I am Bronte Hales, the coordinator. Keep the plan in front of you while I explain how the rooms connect and where equipment is located.\nBronte Hales: North is at the top. You entered through the main door on the southern wall. Reception is immediately to your right, and the broad clean walkway runs straight north from the entrance.\nBronte Hales: The wash-up sink is fixed on the western wall, halfway along the building. Opposite it, on the eastern wall, is the first-aid point. These are useful anchors when the studio is busy.\nBronte Hales: The clay store is in the enclosed north-west corner, beyond the wash-up sink and against both the north and west walls.\nBronte Hales: Do not confuse it with the open reclaim bay immediately south of the sink. Unopened bags of clay are kept behind the corner-room door.\nBronte Hales: From the entrance, walk north along the clean walkway and turn left before the central worktables. The pottery wheels occupy the western side beside those tables.\nBronte Hales: The space closer to reception contains lockers, not wheels. The wheel area is the one farther north, where the floor is prepared for wet work.\nBronte Hales: The glaze bench is on the eastern wall, directly south of the first-aid point. The counter immediately beside reception is only for collecting finished work.\nBronte Hales: Continue to the fire door in the north-east corner. The kiln room is on the far side of that door. A ventilated mixing alcove lies before the door.\nBronte Hales: Finally, the drying racks stand just north of the central worktables and west of the clean walkway. They are open shelves rather than an enclosed room.\nBronte Hales: The central aisle must stay clear, including during classes. Trolleys can cross it briefly, but they cannot be parked there because it is our direct route to the emergency exit.\nBronte Hales: You now know the working layout. I will go through one operating rule for each main facility, beginning with the reclaim bay.\nBronte Hales: At clay reclaim, keep scraps damp. Dry sweeping can put fine dust into the air, so use the wet-cleaning tools stored by the sink and close the reclaim tub after use.\nBronte Hales: At the glaze bench, food and drink are not allowed. A covered water bottle may stay in your locker, but it should not be placed on a surface used for glaze materials.\nBronte Hales: The kiln room is restricted to trained staff. Members may leave labelled work on the loading shelf, yet they must not enter to adjust a firing schedule or open a hot kiln.\nBronte Hales: At the pottery wheels, operate the floor pedal only while seated securely. Switch the wheel off before changing a bat or reaching underneath to retrieve a tool.\nBronte Hales: Every piece placed on the drying racks needs the owner’s name and the date. The date helps us move work fairly when shelf space is limited.\nBronte Hales: If a spill occurs, tell the duty technician. A wet clay spill can usually be contained quickly, but powdered materials require the studio’s specific clean-up procedure.\nBronte Hales: Finished glazed pieces appear on the collection counter after cooling and inspection. Check the matching shelf number rather than moving another member’s work to search for yours.\nBronte Hales: Children may attend the weekend hand-building class, but the wheel area and kiln room remain outside that programme. A tutor will show families their assigned tables.\nBronte Hales: Before leaving, wipe your table with the damp cloth provided, return shared tools, and wash your hands. The final person in each class checks that the clean walkway is empty.\nBronte Hales: That concludes the induction. Ask a member of studio staff if you are uncertain, and leave the clean walkway clear when you go.",
