      "instructions": "21-25: Choose the correct letter, A, B or C.\n26-30: Complete the sentences. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS for each answer.",
      "transcript": "Sorcha Venn: Our first proposal compares standard discharge instructions with teach-back, but the tutor said we had not controlled the consultations closely enough.\nIvor Quade: The obvious solution is to use simulation actors. Real patients would be more authentic, although their diagnoses, questions and prior knowledge would vary too much for this small study.\nSorcha Venn: So the main reason for actors is consistency. Each participant can receive the same fictional case and the same initial information before the communication method changes.\nIvor Quade: Exactly. The actors should use a standard prompt sheet, but respond naturally if a student asks a sensible follow-up question.\nSorcha Venn: What should our primary outcome be? Satisfaction is easy to collect, and confidence sounds attractive, but neither shows whether the message was understood.\nIvor Quade: Let us score recall of the warning signs and the follow-up action. Those details matter in the fictional discharge plan and can be checked against a clear rubric.\nSorcha Venn: Agreed. We can keep satisfaction as a secondary measure. It may explain whether students find teach-back awkward, but it should not replace the recall score.\nIvor Quade: For allocation, I originally placed the morning group in the standard condition and the afternoon group in teach-back. That would be simple to organise.\nSorcha Venn: It would also mix the teaching time with the method. We should randomise within each session, using sealed cards prepared before participants arrive.\nIvor Quade: Yes, within-session randomisation is fairer. We will keep equal numbers where possible and record any participant who withdraws before the simulation begins.\nSorcha Venn: Do we need video? It would show eye contact and gestures, but the rubric focuses on spoken explanations and patient responses.\nIvor Quade: Audio is sufficient and less intrusive. More importantly, a second assessor can code a sample of recordings, so we can estimate whether the rubric is applied consistently.\nSorcha Venn: Then the recording is mainly for checking coding reliability, not for producing presentation clips. We should say that clearly in the participant information.\nIvor Quade: Then we will compare our scores before recruitment starts. If the two coders interpret a category differently, the rubric needs clarification rather than an averaged compromise.\nIvor Quade: The pilot consultation lasted nearly twenty minutes because the discharge script contained six medicines and several unrelated lifestyle points.\nSorcha Venn: That is too crowded. A shorter case with two medicines, one warning sign and one follow-up appointment would leave enough room for genuine explanation.\nIvor Quade: So the key pilot change is to shorten the case, rather than asking actors to speak faster. The communication method should remain the feature we are comparing.\nSorcha Venn: We also need to define what the actor says after the student gives the discharge explanation. A vague request such as repeat that could produce very different responses.\nIvor Quade: A better prompt is: tell me your home plan. That invites the actor to explain what they will do without copying the student’s exact wording.\nSorcha Venn: The rubric should credit accurate content even when the actor pauses and corrects an initial mistake. We can add a self-correction category instead of scoring only the first response.\nIvor Quade: Good. That separates a final misunderstanding from a mistake the actor recognises and repairs after thinking.\nSorcha Venn: Immediate recall might exaggerate performance because the explanation is still fresh. How long can we wait without making the session impractical?\nIvor Quade: Twenty minutes is manageable. Participants can complete the satisfaction form during the interval, but they must not see the discharge sheet again.\nSorcha Venn: On the coding sheet, the category hidden from the recall assessor is labelled communication method. This prevents expectations from influencing the judgement.\nIvor Quade: We can remove the opening section of each recording and label every file with a neutral code. The remaining audio can be scored without revealing which version was assigned to that student.\nSorcha Venn: We should exclude anyone who helped design the fictional case, because they would know the intended details before the consultation.\nSorcha Venn: Who should we recruit? First-year students have little placement experience, while final-year students may already use teach-back routinely.\nIvor Quade: Second-year students offer a useful middle point. They have practised discharge conversations, yet the technique is unlikely to be automatic.\nIvor Quade: That gives us a manageable protocol. We will revise the documents tonight, test the materials with two volunteers on Thursday, and resolve any practical problems before participant recruitment begins next week.",
