      "instructions": "31-36: Complete the lecture notes. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer.\n37-40: Choose the correct interpretation, A-E, for each proxy.",
      "transcript": "Dr Elspeth Rennie: Today I will introduce testate amoebae as one line of evidence for reconstructing past peatland moisture. These are microscopic single-celled organisms that form a durable outer covering.\nDr Elspeth Rennie: When an organism dies, its soft tissues decay, but the shell can remain in peat. Identification therefore relies on durable structures rather than the living cell itself.\nDr Elspeth Rennie: Different species tolerate different moisture conditions. Some are common where the water table lies close to the surface; others occur more often in comparatively dry microhabitats.\nDr Elspeth Rennie: A sample contains an assemblage, not a single perfect indicator. Researchers interpret the relative abundance of several taxa because one shell may have moved, broken or been misidentified.\nDr Elspeth Rennie: Fieldwork begins with a peat core taken through the accumulated deposit. The core preserves a vertical sequence, with recent material near the top and older material below, provided disturbance is recognised.\nDr Elspeth Rennie: In the cited peat-core study, testate-amoeba samples were taken every five centimetres. This spacing belongs to that proxy workflow and should not be confused with the denser schedule used for other evidence.\nDr Elspeth Rennie: Each testate-amoeba sample was prepared so the shells could be examined under a microscope. The analyst recorded enough individuals to describe the assemblage rather than reporting the first few seen.\nDr Elspeth Rennie: In the cited peat-core method, analysts used a light microscope and aimed to count a minimum of 100 tests in each sample whenever possible.\nDr Elspeth Rennie: Counts from an old core cannot by themselves provide water depth. Researchers first need reference data from present-day peat surfaces where both assemblages and current hydrological conditions are measured.\nDr Elspeth Rennie: That modern calibration links changes in community composition with observed water-table depth. Statistical relationships summarise the pattern across many modern samples, not just one convenient site.\nDr Elspeth Rennie: A transfer function applies the modern relationship to fossil assemblages. Its output is an estimate of past water-table depth, often accompanied by an uncertainty range.\nDr Elspeth Rennie: The estimate is not a direct measurement from the past. It assumes that the ecological relationship captured by the calibration remains informative for the older community.\nDr Elspeth Rennie: Now consider the sequence as a whole. A sustained shift towards moisture-associated taxa may suggest a higher local water table, while a brief change in one poorly counted sample deserves caution.\nDr Elspeth Rennie: Testate amoebae mainly inform us about conditions at or near the peat surface. They are therefore valuable indicators of local water-table position, but they do not describe every process around the bog.\nDr Elspeth Rennie: Transfer functions have limitations. A fossil sample may contain a combination unlike the modern training set, so a numerical result can appear precise while resting on a weak analogue. Researchers should report analogue quality instead of allowing a smooth graph to conceal this weakness.\nDr Elspeth Rennie: Taxonomic decisions matter too. Two analysts may divide a variable group differently, and damaged shells sometimes remove the very features used for identification. Counting effort must also be recorded because a small total exaggerates the influence of uncommon taxa.\nDr Elspeth Rennie: Independent evidence can reveal whether an apparent moisture shift forms part of a broader environmental change. The comparison works only if each proxy is interpreted at the scale it actually represents. Agreement is informative, while disagreement can expose a mistaken chronology or an overly simple ecological explanation.\nDr Elspeth Rennie: Pollen is a classical proxy that directly identifies vegetation shifts. It provides a different line of evidence from the testate-amoeba reconstruction, so agreement between them can strengthen an interpretation without making the two records interchangeable.\nDr Elspeth Rennie: The study design also distinguishes the spacing of samples. Pollen, microscopic charcoal and testate amoebae were sampled at five-centimetre intervals, while other evidence was examined more continuously.\nDr Elspeth Rennie: Plant macrofossils and macroscopic charcoal were analysed continuously at one-centimetre intervals. That denser sequence can reveal short-lived changes that a wider interval might miss, although chronology is still required before any change is assigned an age.\nDr Elspeth Rennie: So, for plant macrofossils, the relevant feature here is continuous one-centimetre sampling. Do not confuse that method with the five-centimetre schedule used for pollen, microscopic charcoal and testate amoebae in the same study.\nDr Elspeth Rennie: The core chronology was built independently from dated material and an age-depth model. This matters because depth alone is not an exact calendar age: accumulation rates can vary, and a physical sequence must be dated before its ecological changes are placed in time.\nDr Elspeth Rennie: A second activated study used lipid biomarkers as chemical evidence. It compared peat from fixed depths in cores taken from natural, degraded and restored bogs, while keeping the conclusions bounded by the available sampling design.\nDr Elspeth Rennie: In that study, lipid biomarkers were used to assess peatland composition, decomposition and restoration success. The authors also warned that one core per site and only three sampled depths limited both spatial and vertical resolution.\nDr Elspeth Rennie: The general lesson is methodological restraint: combine biological and chemical proxies, preserve an explicit chronology, and report sampling limits instead of presenting a reconstruction as a direct measurement of the past.",
