      "part": 7,
      "skill": "reading",
      "title": "Academic Reading — Passage 3: Reading a Hospital Queue from Imperfect Records",
      "passageTitle": "Reading a Hospital Queue from Imperfect Records",
      "sectionStyle": "reading",
      "instructions": "Read the passage and answer the questions. Follow the instruction and word limit shown for each item.",
      "passage": "A Electronic medical records can shorten outpatient service by removing manual chart delivery. A study of three Korean public hospitals examined this possibility by comparing waiting times before and after system introduction. The researchers used queueing theory to turn records of arrivals and completed consultations into estimates of waiting. The task was not merely computational. Hospital databases had been created for administration, so recorded times did not always correspond neatly to events required by a queueing model. The study therefore combined digital logs with direct observation and rules for interpreting measurements. It aimed to compare the two periods, not to describe every part of hospital care.\n\nB The selected model was M/M/1. Its two M symbols represent exponential assumptions for interarrival and service times, while the final 1 indicates a single server. The model uses an arrival rate, written lambda, and a service rate, written mu. From these parameters it estimates the average number of patients waiting, the average number in the whole system, the average queueing time and the average total time in the system. These measures link demand and capacity within the assumptions. The study used them for a narrow purpose: comparing outpatient consultation waiting before and after electronic records were introduced, rather than representing every hospital activity.\n\nC Digital timestamps presented an obstacle. Nurses sometimes entered several bookings together after consultations, so a reception time could differ from the patient's arrival. Doctors could also enter prescriptions after payment or without a matching booking. Simply deleting every distorted record would reduce consultation counts and could underestimate waiting and queue size. Grouped reception entries, however, need not change the average interarrival time: one artificially short interval is balanced by a longer one. The researchers therefore derived arrival rate from reception times across the period rather than treating every timestamp as exact. Patient names and gender were masked; reception and prescription logs were recorded to the second.\n\nD Lunch created another distortion. Consultations ran from nine until five, but the hour from 12.30 to 1.30 was excluded and few receptions occurred then. Treating the full eight-hour span as continuous activity would inflate average interarrival time and depress arrival rate. The researchers separated morning data from afternoon data before calculating the intervals, then checked the result against average arrivals in those periods. This adjustment illustrates the study's central caution: a database field becomes useful evidence only after its relationship to real workflow is understood. A correct calculation can still answer the wrong question when inactive time is counted as operating time.\n\nE Estimating service rate required a proxy because databases lacked explicit consultation start and finish fields. Prescription entry was treated as the consultation's end. When a doctor saw patients consecutively, the previous prescription time could represent the next patient's start, making the interval between entries an estimate of service time. That logic failed when patients were not consecutive. Observers therefore recorded when patients entered and left the doctor's room. Their sample was too small to supply the rate alone, so it played a supporting role: the maximum observed consultation became a threshold for removing implausibly long digital intervals. The remaining times were averaged to calculate service rate.\n\nF Estimates showed shorter waits after electronic records were introduced in all three hospitals. Average queueing time in Hospital A fell from 221.75 to 49.33 seconds; Hospital B from 94.23 to 50.87; and Hospital C from 177.64 to 98.49. Across all three, the average changed from 150.62 to 60.34 seconds, a reduction of 59.94 per cent. Observation data also showed decreases and checked the direction of the digital result. These figures describe these hospitals; they do not establish that every installation will produce the same reduction. The model quantified a before-and-after association where manual chart handling had occupied much of the delay.\n\nG The authors identified reasons for caution. The hospitals had relatively small outpatient numbers, so substantial queues were uncommon. Removing manual chart management could therefore account for a large share of the improvement. Only six before-and-after datasets from three institutions were available for testing service rates, and more would be needed for strong generalisation. Hospital size might also alter the result. Large hospitals commonly use appointments, and chart delivery may represent a smaller fraction of waiting; an electronic system could then produce a smaller reduction. These limits narrow the claim to the studied organisations, workflow and period. Extending it requires more data, not automatic transfer of the percentages.\n\nH The contribution is methodological. Observation can capture real consultation events, but collecting it is time-consuming and may cover too few cases. Digital records offer greater volume, yet timestamps can mislead when staff enter information in batches or workflow includes inactive periods. By combining a queueing model, interpreted logs and limited observation, the researchers sought to reduce both problems. Their conclusion was not that M/M/1 completely portrays hospital care. Rather, arrival and service rates extracted with attention to local procedures can support comparisons of waiting times and help identify bottlenecks. The example shows why a model's inputs, assumptions and scope matter as much as its calculation.",
